            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to bridle, then the Latin for a whip."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: equitō, equitāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "equitō, equitāre — \"to ride (on horseback)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mīles per campum equitat. The soldier rides across the plain."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "equitō, equitāre, equitāvī, equitātum. English equitation comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "equitō is built on eques 'horseman' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short), which comes from equus 'horse'. English equitation comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
