          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "calcitrō, calcitrāre — \"to kick (with the heels); to resist\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mūlus calcitrat, et servus fugit. The mule kicks, and the slave runs away."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A first-conjugation verb; the perfect is rare. English recalcitrant comes from recalcitrāre 'to kick back'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "calcitrō is built on calx, calcis 'heel' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): to strike out with the heels. English recalcitrant comes from the compound recalcitrāre."
            }
          ]
        },
